Root cause: TLS session resumption. The SSLContext built by TrustedCertificates keeps the JDK client session cache with its default timeout (24 h), so after one proxied request (the client processes the TLS 1.3 NewSessionTicket while reading the response) later connections to the same host:port resume by PSK and checkServerTrusted is never called. The per-handshake validity filter and its cache are bypassed: a request reaches the backend after the only given self-signed certificate (also the presented one) expired, contrary to AC-3 (an expired trusted self-signed certificate gives 502 CONNECT_FAILED) and to the docs' rule that only the given certificates within their validity period at connection time are trusted (AC-7). The same holds for a given certificate crossing notAfter between handshakes (lp3). The proxy's own host-name check still runs on the resumed session's peer certificates, so this is a validity-period gap, not a host-name gap.
